  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "The ACLU of Northern California celebrates a growing grassroots movement against Flock ALPR cameras, noting that Santa Cruz, Mountain View, Santa Clara County, El Cerrito, and over a dozen other California cities have canceled Flock contracts. It also highlights ACLU/EFF's lawsuit against San Jose over warrantless Flock searches, which prompted the city to shorten retention and curb federal data sharing.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "In January, Santa Cruz became the first city in California to cancel a Flock contract, and took the cameras down in March. Mountain View and Santa Clara County have since ended their contracts.",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "the San Francisco Police Department reportedly let out-of-state agencies run more than 1.6 million searches of the city's license plate reader database \u2014in violation of state law\u2014including at least 19 searches marked as related to U.S. Immigratino and Customs Enforcement (ICE).",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "After we filed the lawsuit, the San Jose City Council cut its data retention period from a full year to 30 days and moved to end data sharing with federal agencies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    }
  ],
